Subject: [PATCH 22/25] docs: record the merged branch's structure and how to
 take it apart

The branch carries four groups of commits with different owners and different
lifetimes, and a commit message is the wrong place to say so -- nobody reads
`git log` before rebasing. Write down what each group is, which upstream PR it
belongs to, and the exact `git rebase --onto` that drops group C once PR #56
merges.

The part worth writing down twice is what was deliberately LEFT OUT of group C,
and why: four of PR#56's seven commits are absent and two of the three taken
were reduced, because the rule for this branch was that only code the merge
experiment actually exercised may enter it. Without that table the omissions
read as oversights rather than as a boundary, and the Rust router bigram bug in
particular deserves to be visible -- it is a real unfixed instance of the same
silent failure, kept out of scope only by our backend default.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
